-- title:
--   Both degeneracy images of a C-point come from one place
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $B$ denotes the localisation `Localization.Away ((D : ℕ) : ℤ)`, $C$ denotes the completion `A₁.valuation.Completion` of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with respect to the valuation of a valuation subring $A_1$, $\mathcal O_1$ denotes the subfield [`ValuationSubring.ratClosure A₁`](def/ValuationSubring_CompletionRatClosure.html#L13) of $C$, and $j : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q}$ denotes the morphism induced by the ring map $\overline{\mathbb Q} \to C$ (the completion coercion after `(WithVal.equiv A₁.valuation).symm`).
--
--   *Numerical data.* $N$ is a nonzero squarefree natural number, $q$ and $q'$ are primes with $q \nmid N$, $q' \nmid N$, $q' \neq q$ and $5 \le q$, $5 \le q'$, and $D$ is a nonzero natural number divisible by $2Nqq'$.
--
--   *The place above $q'$.* $A_1$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q'$ a nonunit of $A_1$ (`LiesOverPrime`), whose decomposition subgroup over $\mathbb Q$ acts isometrically for `A₁.valuation`, and $v_1$ is a height-one prime of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ containing $q'$.
--
--   *The definite quaternion side (group (b)).* Rationals $a_2, b_2$ satisfy `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt q`: $a_2 < 0$, $b_2 < 0$, and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_2,b_2] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly at the place $v$ containing $q$. In $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_2,b_2]$, $\Lambda_2$ is a maximal order, $R_2$ is an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$) with $R_2 \le \Lambda_2$; $n_2$ is a finite-idelic unit lying in `primeHeckeSet R₂ q'`; the order $R_2 \sqcap R_2^{n_2} =$ `meetOrder R₂ n₂` is Eichler of level $Nq'$ and is preserved by conjugation by $n_2$; translation by $n_2$ on the double-coset class set of the finite-idelic stabiliser of `meetOrder R₂ n₂` is an involution; the relevant class sets are finite (with decidable equality for that of $R_2$); and `hlaws₂ : ClassSetHeckeLaws N q' Λ₂ R₂ n₂` asserts, in four clauses, that the edge Hecke matrices commute pairwise, that the vertex Hecke matrices commute pairwise, that for every prime $\ell \neq q'$ the two degeneracy pushforwards intertwine the edge and vertex Hecke operators, and that the joint kernel of those pushforwards is stable under all edge Hecke operators.
--
--   *The indefinite quaternion side.* Rationals $a,b$ satisfy `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q q'`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly at the places containing $q$ or $q'$; and $\Lambda$ is a maximal order in $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$.
--
--   *Coarse moduli over $B$.* $Fbar$ is a field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ which is a curve over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (principal divisors, finite residue extensions, Kähler module free of rank one) and essentially of finite type; $\pi_X : X \to \operatorname{Spec} B$ is a morphism of schemes, $\bar s : \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q} \to \operatorname{Spec} B$ a point, and `pt` assigns to every commutative ring $S$, every $s : \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec} B$ and every fake elliptic curve of level $N$ with $\Lambda$-action over $S$ a morphism to $X$ over $s$; the hypotheses `pt_iso`, `pt_pullback`, `pt_surjective`, `pt_injective` state that `pt` is constant on isomorphism classes, compatible with base change along ring maps, and bijective on isomorphism classes over algebraically closed fields — that is, the four point-theoretic clauses of a coarse moduli space, without the universal property.
--
--   *Curve model and Hecke tower.* $\mathfrak M$ is a `CurveModel` of $Fbar$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (an integral, proper, smooth relative-dimension-one scheme with function field identified with $Fbar$ and closed points in bijection with the places), $e_{\mathfrak M} : \mathfrak M.C \to X \times_{B} \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is an isomorphism with $e_{\mathfrak M}$ followed by the second projection equal to $\mathfrak M.\mathrm{toBase}$, and `gal` is a homomorphism from $\operatorname{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ to the semilinear automorphisms of $Fbar$. Further, $\mathbb T$ is a `HeckeTower.TowerData q q' Fbar`, giving for each prime $\ell \notin \{q,q'\}$ a curve field $\mathbb T.F_\ell$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ together with two finite integral $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra maps $\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,i)$, $i \in \{0,1\}$, from $Fbar$; `galT`, `W`, `WT` are the corresponding semilinear automorphism data; and `tw` is a witness of type `ModuliTowerWitnessD Λ N q q' D Fbar X πX sbar pt 𝔐 e𝔐 gal 𝕋 galT W WT`, which attaches to each place of $Fbar$ a fake elliptic curve over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ whose `pt`-image is the point of $X \times_B \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q}$ determined by the place through $\mathfrak M.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}$ and $e_{\mathfrak M}$, attaches to each place of $\mathbb T.F_\ell$ a fake elliptic curve with extra level $\ell$ in a manner bijective up to isomorphism, requires `gal` and `galT` to induce the given automorphism on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and `W`, `WT` to be trivial on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, relates restriction of places along $\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,0)$ to forgetting the extra level, and imposes further compatibilities.
--
--   *Matrix data at $q'$.* $\iota_1$ is an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_2,b_2] \to M_2(\mathcal O_1)$, $\rho_1$ is the induced homomorphism $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_2,b_2]^\times \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(\mathcal O_1)$ (hypothesis `hρ₁` identifies $\rho_1$ with $\iota_1$ followed by the projective quotient), $\varpi_1$ is a pseudo-uniformiser of $\mathcal O_1$ in $C$ whose underlying element maps to $q'$ in $C$, and the holomorphic ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ₁ ρ₁` is a domain.
--
--   *Hecke elements and arithmetic groups (group (g)).* For each prime $\ell \notin\{q,q'\}$, $s_1(\ell) \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_2,b_2]^\times$ and an idelic unit $sf_1(\ell)$ satisfy `hs₁`: $sf_1(\ell)$ has component $s_1(\ell)\otimes 1$ at every finite place not containing $q'$ and component $1$ at the places containing $q'$; the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $sf_1(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₂ (meetOrder R₂ n₂) ℓ` if $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₂ n₂) ℓ` otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_1(\ell)) = \ell$. The subgroups $\Gamma_1$ are prescribed by `hΓ₁0` — $\Gamma_1(\mathrm{none})$ consists of the units lying in `CosetGraph.awayUnits R₂ v₁` (locally in the unit group of the local box of $R_2$ at every finite place other than $v_1$) whose reduced norm has even $q'$-adic valuation — and by `hΓ₁ℓ`: $\Gamma_1(\ell) = \Gamma_1(\mathrm{none}) \sqcap s_1(\ell)\Gamma_1(\mathrm{none})s_1(\ell)^{-1}$. The elements $w_1$ satisfy `hw₁`: $w_1(\mathrm{none})$ lies in `awayUnits R₂ v₁` and has reduced norm $q'$, and $w_1(\ell)$ lies in `awayUnits (meetOrder R₂ (sf₁ ℓ)) v₁` with reduced norm $q'$. The elements $\bar w_1$ satisfy `hwbar₁`: each has reduced norm $q$, is a unit of the local box of $R_2$ (respectively of `meetOrder R₂ (sf₁ ℓ)`) at every finite place $u \neq v_1$ not containing $q$, and normalises both that local box and the local box of $\Lambda_2$ at every $u \neq v_1$.
--
--   *Local Galois data.* $dIso_1$ sends the decomposition subgroup of $A_1$ over $\mathbb Q$ to isometric automorphisms of $C$ realising the natural action (`hdIso₁`), and $\chi$ is a character of that decomposition subgroup with values in $\mathbb Z/2$ which is trivial on inertia (`hχ₁`), nontrivial on every Frobenius at $q'$ (`hχ₂`), and whose kernel consists exactly of the elements fixing every $x$ in the residue field of $A_1$ with $x^{q'^2} = x$ (`hχ₃`).
--
--   *Coefficient rings.* $\mathcal O_0$ is a characteristic-zero discrete valuation domain with irreducible element $\pi$, $\pi$-adically complete, with residue field of cardinality $q'$ and $(q') = (\pi)$, equipped with an algebra structure making $\mathcal O_1$ its fraction field; $Onr$ is a characteristic-zero $\mathcal O_0$-domain with an $\mathcal O_0$-automorphism $Fr$, complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$, with that ideal maximal, every element integral modulo $\pi$, every monic polynomial of positive degree having a root modulo $\pi$, and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{q'} \pmod{\pi}$. Moreover `vdet` is a homomorphism from $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathcal O_1)$ to $\mathbb Z$ characterised by `hvdet` as the $\pi$-valuation of the determinant up to units of $\mathcal O_0$; $\psi_0 : Onr \to C$ is a ring map of valuation at most $1$ compatible with $\mathcal O_0 \to \mathcal O_1 \to C$; $\pi$ maps to $\varpi_1.\varpi$; $N$ is a unit of $\mathcal O_0$; the image of $\mathcal O_0$ in $\mathcal O_1$ is exactly the set of elements of valuation at most $1$; and `hrk` requires that for $x$ of valuation $<1$ and $y \neq 0$ in $C$ some power $v(x)^n$ is at most $v(y)$.
--
--   *Integral models over $\mathcal O_0$.* $f_0 : \mathcal X_0 \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ together with $pt_0$ satisfies `IsCoarseModuli Λ N 𝒳₀ f₀ pt₀` (the four point-theoretic clauses and the universal property), with $\mathcal X_0$ integral and $f_0$ flat, separated, locally of finite type and quasi-compact; for each prime $\ell \notin \{q',q\}$, $g_0(\ell) : \mathcal Y_0(\ell) \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ together with $ptT_0(\ell)$ satisfies `IsCoarseModuliT Λ N ℓ`; $d_{0,0}(\ell)$ and $d_{1,0}(\ell)$ are morphisms $\mathcal Y_0(\ell)\to \mathcal X_0$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ which on moduli points forget the extra level, respectively send a pair to any fake elliptic curve $\ell$-isogenous to it in the sense of `IsLevelIsogeny`; and $ar$, $arbar$ are endomorphisms of $\mathcal X_0$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ which on moduli points realise the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $q'$, respectively at $q$.
--
--   *Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation.* $\Theta$ and, for each $\ell$, $\Theta_T(\ell)$ assign to every $\mathcal O_0$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent a map from the product of the functor corepresented by $Onr$ with Drinfeld's functor `FormalOmega.Omega 𝒪₁ π` of Deligne data to the nilpotent points of $f_0$, respectively of $g_0(\ell)$. The hypothesis `hCD` is a conjunction of twelve clauses: naturality of $\Theta$ and of each $\Theta_T(\ell)$ in $B$; invariance of $\Theta$ under the twisted action (`OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct π Onr Fr vdet`) of the image under $\iota_1$ of any $\gamma \in$ `awayUnits R₂ v₁`, and of $\Theta_T(\ell)$ under that action for $\gamma$ in the intersection of `awayUnits R₂ v₁` with its conjugate by $s_1(\ell)$; for every algebraically closed $\mathcal O_0$-field $k$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi : Onr \to k$, surjectivity of $P \mapsto \Theta(\psi,P)$ onto the nilpotent points of $f_0$ with fibres exactly the orbits of that twisted action, and the analogous statement for $\Theta_T(\ell)$ with the intersected group; the initiality of $\Theta$ and of each $\Theta_T(\ell)$ among natural invariant transformations into the nilpotent points of an arbitrary $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ (existence and uniqueness of the factorisation); compatibility of $\Theta_T(\ell)$ with $d_{0,0}(\ell)$ (the composite is $\Theta$) and with $d_{1,0}(\ell)$ (the composite applied to $x$ is $\Theta$ of any $x'$ carried to $x$ by the twisted action of $s_1(\ell)$); and compatibility of $\Theta$ with $ar$ (composition twists the $Onr$-component by $Fr$) and with $arbar$ (composition corresponds to the twisted action of $\bar w_1(\mathrm{none})$). In addition, the base changes along $\mathcal O_0 \to \mathcal O_1 \to C$ of $f_0$ and of every $g_0(\ell)$ are integral schemes.
--
--   *Comparison over $C$.* $u$ is an isomorphism from $X \times_B \operatorname{Spec} C$ (the pullback of $\pi_X$ along $j$ followed by $\bar s$) onto $\mathcal X_0 \times_{\mathcal O_0} \operatorname{Spec} C$, compatible with the projections to $\operatorname{Spec} C$ (`hu`) and with the two moduli-point maps: for every ring $S$, every $s : \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec} C$ and every fake elliptic curve $E$ over $S$, the lift of $\mathrm{pt}(E)$ and $s$ followed by $u$ equals the lift of $\mathrm{pt}_0(E)$ and $s$ (`hupt`).
--
--   Write $\theta$ for the composite of $u^{-1}$, the canonical morphism $X\times_B \operatorname{Spec} C \to X \times_B \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (the lift of the first projection and of the second projection followed by $j$), and $e_{\mathfrak M}^{-1}$; thus $\theta : \mathcal X_0\times_{\mathcal O_0}\operatorname{Spec} C \to \mathfrak M.C$. For a place $R$ of $Fbar$ write $x_R := (\mathfrak M.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}^{-1} R).1$ for the corresponding $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point of $\mathfrak M.C$.
--
--   *The fixed prime and the point.* $\ell$ is a prime distinct from $q$ and $q'$; $g_0$ at the index $\ell$ viewed as a prime away from $q'$ and $q$ is locally of finite type and separated. The morphisms $dC_0$ and $dC_1$ from $\mathcal Y_0(\ell)\times_{\mathcal O_0}\operatorname{Spec} C$ to $\mathcal X_0\times_{\mathcal O_0}\operatorname{Spec} C$ are the base changes to $C$ of $d_{0,0}(\ell)$ and $d_{1,0}(\ell)$: each commutes with the first projection up to the corresponding degeneracy map and preserves the second projection (`hdC₀`, `hdC₀'`, `hdC₁`, `hdC₁'`), and each sends the generic point to the generic point (`hdom₀`, `hdom₁`). Finally $Q$ is a place of $Fbar$, $y' : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \mathcal Y_0(\ell)\times_{\mathcal O_0}\operatorname{Spec} C$ is a section of the second projection, and the hypothesis `hyQ` states
--   $$y' \text{ followed by } dC_0 \text{ and } \theta \;=\; j \text{ followed by } x_Q .$$
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exists a place $P$ of $\mathbb T.F_\ell$ such that both of the following hold. First, $y'$ followed by $dC_0$ and by $\theta$ equals $j$ followed by $x_{R_0}$, where $R_0 = P.\mathrm{restrictAlong}\,(\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,0))\,(\mathbb T.\mathrm{integral}(\ell,0))$ is the place of $Fbar$ obtained by pulling back the valuation subring of $P$ along $\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,0)$. Second, $y'$ followed by $dC_1$ and by $\theta$ equals $j$ followed by $x_{R_1}$, where $R_1 = P.\mathrm{restrictAlong}\,(\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,1))\,(\mathbb T.\mathrm{integral}(\ell,1))$ is the pullback of $P$ along $\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,1)$. In particular the first conjunct, read against `hyQ`, exhibits $Q$ as the restriction of $P$ along $\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,0)$.
--
--   This is the point-theoretic comparison step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld analysis on the $q'$-side: a $C$-valued point of the integral model with extra level $\ell$, whose image under the first degeneracy map corresponds to a place $Q$ of the function field of the curve model, is shown to come from a single place $P$ of the level-$\ell$ function field $\mathbb T.F_\ell$, the two degeneracy images being the points attached to the two restrictions of $P$ along $\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,0)$ and $\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell,1)$. It is used in the proof that the set of places obtained in this way is dense, which in turn supports the identification of the Hecke action on the tower with the combinatorial action on the class sets of the definite quaternion order.
